can cover many points across columns. So we can assign each sunny line to cover one point in each uncovered column, but we need to cover all points in each column. For column i with size s ≤ k, we need to cover s points. Since we have k sunny lines, we can assign each of the s points to a distinct sunny line, and the remaining k-s sunny lines can be unused for that column. So it's possible. However, we need to ensure that the points assigned to a given sunny line across different columns are collinear (i.e., lie on the same line). That's the main difficulty: we cannot arbitrarily assign points to sunny lines; they must lie on a common line. So the above argument is insufficient.\n\nThus we need a more refined bound.\n\nThe known solution likely uses a counting argument based on slopes. Let's try to derive a bound on k.\n\nConsider the set of points S. For each line, define its slope (including infinite for vertical). Non-sunny slopes are 0, ∞, -1. Sunny slopes are all others. Suppose we have k sunny lines. Then there are at most k distinct sunny slopes (since each line has a slope). Actually each sunny line could have a different slope, but we have at most k sunny lines, so at most k sunny slopes. The non-sunny lines have slopes among {0,∞,-1}, but there could be multiple lines with same slope (e.g., multiple vertical lines). So total number of distinct slopes among all lines is at most k + 3 (if we have at least one line of each non-sunny slope). But we can have many vertical lines, all same slope ∞.\n\nNow consider the set of points S. For each point (a,b), consider the slope of the line connecting it to the origin (0,0). That slope is b/a. For points in S, b/a ranges from something to something. But maybe we can use a combinatorial argument: For each point (a,b), consider the line through that point with slope -1 (i.e., line x+y = a+b). That line is non-sunny. So each point lies on a diagonal line of slope -1. But we have only at most one diagonal line (if we use